  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nRiver valley fog has already gained traction across the\nsoutheastern portions of the forecast area this morning, with a\nstrengthening signal on satellite imagery of additional\nformation around the Ohio River Valley. Sub-freezing\ntemperatures at the time of writing will continue to drop\nthrough the predawn hours, yielding potential for freezing fog\nalong the majority of our river basins this morning. An SPS was\nissued this past evening to highlight concerns for dense fog and\nfreezing fog as a result. As further establishment of fog occurs\nover the next few hours, a dense fog advisory may be warranted,\nespecially in the event of slick roadways caused by the freezing\nfog.\n\nFog erosion will slowly transpire after sunrise this morning,\ngiving way to clearing skies for the rest of today. More river\nvalley fog is possible during the overnight hours tonight and\nWednesday night.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nExpansive high pressure at the forefront of this week's weather\nwill continue to yield a long stretch of pleasant conditions\nacross the Central Appalachians. Cold mornings give way to warm\nafternoons within this regime, with daily increases in diurnal\nswings through the work week. Additionally, deep layers of dry\nair noted in forecast soundings mixing down during the day will\nyield lowering dew points and minimum RH values each afternoon.\nBy Thursday, minRH values as low as the mid-teens could be\nobserved along the spine of the Appalachians. While the wind\ncomponent will be lacking to suggest fire weather concerns, \nthis will remain closely monitored each day for potential \nheightened messaging.\n\nThe dominating upper level ridge pattern begins to break down as\nwe near the weekend, opening the door for upstream disturbances\nto stretch across the country. Long range forecast models\nsuggest rain out ahead of a cold front could approach the Ohio\nRiver Valley by the balance of the weekend.",
